WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.455
Hi, I'm Samosa!

00:00:01.455 --> 00:00:04.135
Grand Theft Auto Five now runs in Chrome.

00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:05.476
Not streamed, not emulated.

00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:06.481
Here is how.

00:00:08.058 --> 00:00:12.225
The idea: compile the game's own engine to sixty-four bit WebAssembly, and

00:00:12.225 --> 00:00:15.698
let its threads reach the browser only through shared memory.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:20.190
The engine runs as real threads that block and wait.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.179
They never call the browser.

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:24.061
They write requests into shared memory.

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:26.027
The page drops keys in there too.

00:00:27.681 --> 00:00:30.469
Small workers pick them up: graphics, files, sound.

00:00:31.775 --> 00:00:33.090
Graphics were not rewritten.

00:00:34.086 --> 00:00:38.277
The engine still talks to Direct3D eleven, but the device is a fake.

00:00:38.697 --> 00:00:41.324
Each call becomes a small command in a ring buffer.

00:00:41.744 --> 00:00:45.446
A worker that owns the WebGPU device replays them in order.

00:00:45.866 --> 00:00:48.601
Every shader was translated ahead of time.

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:53.290
Geometry and tessellation shaders have no WebGPU stage, so they are dropped.

00:00:54.633 --> 00:00:58.217
When the engine reads a file, it fills a slot in memory and waits.

00:00:58.637 --> 00:01:02.124
A worker checks a block store in the browser's private file system.

00:01:02.544 --> 00:01:05.527
Missing blocks come from the server, many requests at once.

00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:08.785
The bytes land in the engine's heap, and the thread wakes.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.441
That is how about twenty-one gigabytes of game data can sit behind a tab.

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:17.103
Only the blocks the world needs are ever fetched.

00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:21.509
I ran it on a server with no graphics card.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:24.860
Chrome drew it in software, slowly, but this is the real world.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.110
Same engine, same data, inside a browser tab.

00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:33.684
The lesson: shared memory and atomics let blocking engine threads live beside

00:01:33.684 --> 00:01:35.810
a browser that only speaks async.

00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:42.193
The real engine, Direct3D replayed on WebGPU, and files streamed in blocks.

00:01:42.613 --> 00:01:44.870
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:45.515
I'm Samosa.

00:01:45.515 --> 00:01:45.838
Bye!
